  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 14: DESIGN CHALLENGE - AVOIDING CENTRAL BOTTLENECKS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="challenge-bottlenecks"
    eyebrow="DISTRIBUTED CONCEPTS"
    title="Challenge 2: Avoiding Single Bottlenecks"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="One Central Login Server" badge="Bottleneck 1" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Having one machine handle all authentication.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          Works for 100 users, but crashes when 100,000 try to log in at once. <em>Solution:</em> Run multiple login servers behind a load balancer.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="One Giant Database Disk" badge="Bottleneck 2" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Storing all user records on a single hard drive.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          The disk becomes too slow to process all read/write requests. <em>Solution:</em> Shard data across multiple database servers.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Centralized Algorithms" badge="Bottleneck 3" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Algorithms that require knowing the state of all nodes.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
          Asking 10,000 servers for their status creates huge network traffic. <em>Solution:</em> Have nodes communicate only with nearby neighbors.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how decentralization and sharding allow websites to scale without crashing under heavy traffic.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 15: DESIGN CHALLENGE - HANDLING FAILURES
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="challenge-failures"
    eyebrow="DISTRIBUTED CONCEPTS"
    title="Challenge 3: Handling Machine Crashes Automatically"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          In large systems with thousands of servers, hardware failures happen every day. A distributed system is designed to keep running even when individual servers crash.
        </p>

        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 uppercase">How Systems Recover from Crashes:</h4>
          <ol class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-decimal font-medium">
            <li><strong>Heartbeats</strong>: Servers send regular "I am alive" messages. If a server stops answering, the system marks it as failed.</li>
            <li><strong>Data Replication</strong>: Important records are saved on 3 separate servers so losing one causes zero data loss.</li>
            <li><strong>Circuit Breakers</strong>: If a database slows down, the system stops sending new requests to it temporarily so the whole website does not freeze.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Heartbeat",
              definition: "A periodic check message proving a server is still running."
            },
            {
              term: "Circuit Breaker",
              definition: "A software pattern that stops sending requests to a broken service to prevent system-wide crashes."
            },
            {
              term: "Failover",
              definition: "Automatically redirecting user traffic to a backup server when the primary server fails."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Use the electrical circuit breaker analogy: an electrical breaker trips so your whole house does not catch fire when one appliance short-circuits.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

