  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 31: REAL-WORLD LIMITATIONS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="cloud-limitations"
    eyebrow="RISKS & LIMITS"
    title="Real-World Limits and Risks of Cloud Computing"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT 542CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Internet Dependency" badge="Connectivity" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">No internet means no access to your servers.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          If your office loses its internet connection, work stops. Factory robots and medical devices cannot rely entirely on cloud due to network drops.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Vendor Lock-in" badge="Portability" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Hard to switch providers once you build deeply.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Using proprietary features from one provider makes moving to another provider difficult and expensive later.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Noisy Neighbors" badge="Shared Hardware" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Other tenants sharing the same physical machine.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          If another company on your physical server runs a heavy calculation, your server might experience temporary slow-downs.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain why critical systems (like hospital monitors or factory machines) use local edge computers rather than relying purely on cloud.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

